<h2>Understanding Form Types</h2>

<p>
    The utility supports multiple supplemental application formats:
</p>

<h3>Autodetect</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Automatically identifies the form type from the uploaded document</li>
    <li>Best when you're not sure which form type to use</li>
    <li>May take slightly longer as it analyzes the document first</li>
</ul>

<h3>Specific Form Types</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Kinsale</strong> – For Kinsale Insurance supplemental applications</li>
    <li><strong>Amwins</strong> – For Amwins supplemental applications</li>
    <li><strong>CS</strong> – For CS supplemental applications</li>
    <li>Other form types may be available depending on your configuration</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Selecting the correct form type ensures the best field mapping and extraction accuracy.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the form type selector showing all available options. Show a brief description or example for each form type if available. -->

<h2>Update Business Details Option</h2>

<p>
    When enabled, the "Update Business Details" option:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Extracts business information</strong> – Pulls business name, address, operations, and other details from the form</li>
    <li><strong>Updates client record</strong> – Automatically updates the client's business information in Quickfire</li>
    <li><strong>Keeps data current</strong> – Ensures your client records reflect the latest information</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>When to use:</strong>
</p>
<ul>
    <li>When the form contains updated business information</li>
    <li>When you want to keep client records synchronized with form data</li>
    <li>When business details have changed since the last update</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>When to skip:</strong>
</p>
<ul>
    <li>When business details haven't changed</li>
    <li>When you only want to transfer form data, not update records</li>
    <li>When you prefer to update business details manually</li>
</ul>

